This works, right? I think this would avoid an async state machine allocation per request at runtime for truly async route handlers that are return ValueTasks of reference types.

```suggestion
            if (typeArg.IsValueType)
            {
                return Expression.Call(ValueTaskOfTToValueTaskOfObjectMethod.MakeGenericMethod(typeArg), methodCall);
            }

            return methodCall;
```

I don't think this is guaranteed to test the scenario, the TCS is completed before entering the handler. Thinking about it some more it's kind of hard to test this reliably... best I can think of is to set the TCS after `requestDelegate(httpContext);` and maybe adding a `Task.Yield()` after `await tcs.Task;`. Basically trying to force the delegate to be async and verifying that the `requestDelegate` doesn't complete synchronously.
So:
```csharp
var task = requestDelegate(httpContext);
tcs.SetResult();
await task;
```

This isn't waiting for the delegate to complete before returning. You can see the behavior by having the following code:
```csharp
app.MapGet("/task", handler: async (HttpContext c) =>
{
    await Task.Delay(10000);
    c.Response.StatusCode = 400;
})
.AddFilter((c, n) =>
{
    return n(c);
});
```

A request to `/task` will complete immediately with a 200, and after 10 seconds VS will show an ODE in the Debug output window (this will be an unobserved exception since no one is waiting for the completion anymore).

Then, if you remove the filter you will see a request to `/task` will complete after 10 seconds and have a status of 400 as expected.
